{"records":[{"id":"nfjfjjx7bg","title":"Comparative Efficacy of AAV-Tafazzin and AAV-Δ5 Tafazzin in Restoring Mitochondrial Function in Barth Syndrome Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell Derived Cardiomyocytes","description":"We compared efficacy and efficiency of two AAV-Tafazzin variants AAV-CoTAZ (full length) and AAV-Δ5 (exon 5 removed) in Barth syndrome iPSC cardiomyocytes. We performed AAV transduction in iPSC cardiomyocytes and cultured cells for 7 days followed by an array of mitochondrial functional assays and gene expression analysis. The raw data from MTT, ATP, ROS, TMRE, and OCR assays and the qRT-PCR assay for all healthy and BTHS cell lines. Mitochondrial assays are normalized to 1ug of protein for all assays. We hypothesized that the AAV delivery of tafazzin improves mitochondrial function in BTHS iPSC cardiomyocytes and that Δ5 variant performs superior as it is abundant in heart and skeletal muscle.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-29T07:25:49.614Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nfjfjjx7bg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nfjfjjx7bg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sree","last_name":"Venigalla","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Expression","Mitochondrial Disorder"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w7nwbnsjvt","title":"A transcriptomic based deconvolution framework for assessing differentiation stages and drug responses of AML","description":"Count table for LUMC data.\n\nWe have downloaded the non-normalized count matrices (htseq-counts) and the meta files of the four discovery cohorts (TCGA-LAML, BEAT-AML, TARGET-AML and TARGET-ALL) from https://portal.gdc.cancer.gov. For LEUCEGENE, count data was downloaded from their dedicated site (https://data.leucegene.iric.ca/) along with their provided meta data. All meta/count data were pre-processed using R (v4.1.0). For the meta data, genomic aberration labels were relabeled to the main AML WHO 2016 classes, non-AML samples were removed from the down-stream analyses, ELN-classes were relabeled according to ELN 2017 recommendations. For the count data, ERCC spike-ins and mitochondrial genes were removed, and the count matrix was then sorted according genes standard deviation in order to remove the duplicated genes that had less variation thus providing less information, and lastly the gene ensembl ids were converted to gene symbols. Before converting ensembl ids into gene symbols, the stemness score for each patient was calculated via count-per-million (cpm) normalized libraries.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-23T11:15:59.282Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w7nwbnsjvt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w7nwbnsjvt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Onur","last_name":"Karakaslar","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m22mdgmzk2","title":"Cole et al. 2021 Endocrinology Supplementary Data","description":"Tafazzin Deficiency Reduces Basal Insulin Secretion and Mitochondrial Function in Pancreatic Islets from Male Mice \nSupplementary Data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-11T03:28:01.967Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m22mdgmzk2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m22mdgmzk2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Grant Michael","last_name":"Hatch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Endocrinology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nm5wd5t98m","title":"Metformin in SALI ","description":"The gut microbiota imbalance is primarily characterized by an increase in the abundance of succinate-producing bacteria (Prevotellaceae and Bacteroidaceae) and a decrease in succinate-consuming bacteria (Ruminococcaceae and Clostridiaceae) after CLP-induced SALI. This dysbiosis leads to elevated succinate levels in both serum and lung tissue. However, metformin effectively reduces succinate levels by modulating the gut microbiota, which subsequently regulates the polarization of alveolar macrophages (AMs) and exerts anti-inflammatory effects by inhibiting succinate receptor 1 (SUCNR1) and the downstream PI3K/AKT/HIF-1α signaling pathway.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-14T15:37:31.313Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nm5wd5t98m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nm5wd5t98m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhongliang","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sepsis","Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hz3w6zbwvm","title":"TCAIM-OGDH","description":"Western Blot data deposit for the manuscript \"Targeted degradation of the α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase mediated by a mitochondrial DNAJC protein regulates metabolism\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-23T16:39:17.871Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hz3w6zbwvm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hz3w6zbwvm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wen","last_name":"yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2019YFA0508601"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"31871430"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"31900856"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y2kv8m3zvt","title":"source data for Lu et al \"SLC25A51 Decouples the Mitochondrial NAD+/NADH Ratio to control proliferation of AML cells\" ","description":"All Source Data for Lu et al \"SLC25A51 Decouples the Mitochondrial NAD+/NADH Ratio to control proliferation of AML cells\"\ncurrent for 2023-11-29","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-08T07:55:10.815Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y2kv8m3zvt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y2kv8m3zvt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lulu","last_name":"Cambronne","orcid":"0000-0002-3547-448X"},{"first_name":"Mu-Jie","last_name":"Lu","orcid":"0000-0002-2174-1153"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dmph2p5rcc","title":"SPATA2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 by modulating linear ubiquitination","description":"Stimulation of cells with TNFα leads to the formation of the TNF-R1 signaling complex (TNF-RSC) to mediate downstream cellular fate decision. Activation of the TNF-RSC is modulated by different types of ubiquitination and may lead to cell death, including apoptosis and necroptosis, in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners. Spata2 (spermatogenesis-associated 2) is an adaptor protein recruited into the TNF-RSC to modulate the interaction between the linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex (LUBAC) and the deubiquitinase CYLD (cylindromatosis). However, the mechanism by which Spata2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 is unclear. Here, we report that Spata2-deficient cells show resistance to RIPK1-dependent apoptosis and necroptosis and are also partially protected against RIPK1-independent apoptosis. Spata2 deficiency promotes M1 ubiquitination of RIPK1 to inhibit RIPK1 kinase activity. Furthermore, we provide biochemical evidence for the USP domain of CYLD and the PUB domain of the SPATA2 complex preferentially deubiquitinating the M1 ubiquitin chain in vitro. Spata2 deficiency also promotes the activation of MKK4 and JNK and cytokine production independently of RIPK1 kinase activity. Spata2 deficiency sensitizes mice to systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) induced by TNFα, which can be suppressed by RIPK1 inhibitor Nec-1s. Thus, Spata2 can regulate inflammatory response and cell death in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-02T17:40:36.615Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dmph2p5rcc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dmph2p5rcc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ran","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zdh5brnjv5","title":"SPATA2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 by modulating linear ubiquitination","description":"Stimulation of cells with TNFα leads to the formation of the TNF-R1 signaling complex (TNF-RSC) to mediate downstream cellular fate decision. Activation of the TNF-RSC is modulated by different types of ubiquitination and may lead to cell death, including apoptosis and necroptosis, in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners. Spata2 (spermatogenesis-associated 2) is an adaptor protein recruited into the TNF-RSC to modulate the interaction between the linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex (LUBAC) and the deubiquitinase CYLD (cylindromatosis). However, the mechanism by which Spata2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 is unclear. Here, we report that Spata2-deficient cells show resistance to RIPK1-dependent apoptosis and necroptosis and are also partially protected against RIPK1-independent apoptosis. Spata2 deficiency promotes M1 ubiquitination of RIPK1 to inhibit RIPK1 kinase activity. Furthermore, we provide biochemical evidence for the USP domain of CYLD and the PUB domain of the SPATA2 complex preferentially deubiquitinating the M1 ubiquitin chain in vitro. Spata2 deficiency also promotes the activation of MKK4 and JNK and cytokine production independently of RIPK1 kinase activity. Spata2 deficiency sensitizes mice to systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) induced by TNFα, which can be suppressed by RIPK1 inhibitor Nec-1s. Thus, Spata2 can regulate inflammatory response and cell death in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-02T17:39:12.052Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zdh5brnjv5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zdh5brnjv5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ran","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fhw7ff6gd3","title":"Data for: Anticarin β inhibits human glioma progression by suppressing cancer stemness via STAT3","description":"Glioma is the most common form of malignant brain cancer. It is very difficult to cure malignant glioma because of the presence of glioma stem cells, which are a barrier to cure, have high tumorigenesis, associated with drug resistance, and responsible for relapse by regulating stemness genes. In this study, our results demonstrated that anticarin β, a natural compound from Antiaris toxicaria, can effectively and selectively suppress proliferation and cause apoptosis in glioma cells, which has an IC50 that is 100 times lower than that in mouse normal neural stem cells. Importantly, cell sphere formation assay and real time-quantitative analysis reveal that anticarin β inhibits cancer stemness by modulating related stemness gene expression. Additionally, anticarin β induces DNA damage to regulate the oncogene expression of signal transducer and activator of transcription 3 (STAT3), Akt, mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs), and eventually leading to apoptosis. Furthermore, anticarin β effectively inhibits glioma growth and prolongs the lifts pan of tumor-bearing mice without systemic toxicity in the orthotopic xenograft mice model. These results suggest that anticarin β is a promising candidate inhibitor for malignant glioma.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-27T06:55:33.341Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fhw7ff6gd3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fhw7ff6gd3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Glioma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"95wwrptr59","title":"Lipid availability influences ferroptosis sensitivity in cancer cells by regulating polyunsaturated fatty acid trafficking","description":"Ferroptosis is a form of cell death caused by lipid peroxidation that is emerging as a target for cancer therapy, highlighting the need to identify factors that govern ferroptosis susceptibility. Lipid peroxidation occurs primarily on phospholipids containing polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs). Here, we show that even though extracellular lipid limitation reduces cellular PUFA levels, lipid-starved cancer cells are paradoxically more sensitive to ferroptosis. Using mass spectrometry-based lipidomics with stable isotope fatty acid labeling, we show that lipid limitation induces a fatty acid trafficking pathway in which PUFAs are liberated from triglycerides to synthesize highly unsaturated PUFAs such as arachidonic and adrenic acid. These PUFAs then accumulate in phospholipids, including ether phospholipids, to promote ferroptosis sensitivity. Therefore, PUFA levels within cancer cells do not necessarily correlate with ferroptosis susceptibility. Rather, how cancer cells respond to extracellular lipid levels by trafficking PUFAs into proper phospholipid pools contributes to their sensitivity to ferroptosis.  \n\nThis data set includes uncropped Western blot images from this manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-16T17:49:29.291Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/95wwrptr59","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/95wwrptr59"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Evan","last_name":"Lien","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R00CA255928"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"5T32CA251066-03"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":141640,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":131,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":141640,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":140931,"restricted":69,"embargoed":640},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2349},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1399},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":695},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":480},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":138977},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1909},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":905},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":899},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":529},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":382}]}}